@*
    The four MudBlazor providers plus the root-element sync, in one place.

    Every dashboard layout needs exactly this block, and two of the three were getting it wrong.
    DashboardMinimalLayout and DashboardEmptyLayout each rendered a bare <MudThemeProvider /> — no
    Theme, no IsDarkMode — so MudBlazor fell back to `new MudTheme()`: stock Material purple, 4px
    radii, and a type scale whose h1 is 6rem where DashboardTypography asks for 1.75rem. The
    visible result was that a visitor on a dark dashboard who hit /404 or /500 got a white page in
    a colour scheme belonging to no part of the product, on top of the dark <html> the pre-boot
    script had already painted.

    A component rather than a snippet to copy: the failure mode was a layout forgetting two
    attributes, and there is now nothing to forget.
*@
@namespace Zonit.Dashboard.Components.Layouts
@using Zonit.Dashboard.Themes
@implements IDisposable

@inject IThemeManager ThemeManager

<MudThemeProvider Theme="@ThemeManager.Current.MudTheme" IsDarkMode="@ThemeManager.IsDark" />
<MudPopoverProvider />
<MudDialogProvider />
<MudSnackbarProvider />

@* ─── Root element sync ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   DashboardHead runs a pre-boot script that writes background-color and color-scheme straight
   onto <html> as an INLINE STYLE, so the browser paints the right ground before MudBlazor's CSS
   has loaded. That part is worth keeping. What it cannot do is change its mind: an inline style
   outranks every stylesheet, so the value stamped at page load survived every later theme switch.
   Switching to Light left <html> painted #161723 under a light body — measured in the browser —
   which is the "the bottom of the page is a different colour, and in dark mode it is white"
   report, and also why the switch looked like it had not worked at all until a reload.

   One !important declaration, rendered by a component so it re-renders with the theme, takes the
   root back. background-color reads MudThemeProvider's own custom property, so no colour is
   duplicated here to drift; color-scheme is the one value that cannot come from a property, and
   setting it on the root is also what overrides the server-rendered <meta name="color-scheme">
   (which is likewise frozen at whatever the request believed). Scrollbars, form controls and the
   overscroll gutter follow it. *@
@*
   --z-chrome-edge is the other value only this component knows. A dark theme cannot draw its
   elevation ladder with fill — every step in a near-black ramp is worth about 1.1:1, and the
   appbar measured 1.04:1 against the page — so the boundary has to be a drawn line instead. The
   token carries the whole border shorthand so the stylesheet can state the rules statically and
   this one declaration decides whether they paint. There is no class or attribute on <html> to
   key a CSS selector off: MudBlazor writes the palette as custom properties and nothing else, and
   the inline color-scheme the pre-boot script leaves behind is stale by definition. *@
<style>
    html {
        background-color: var(--mud-palette-background, @(ThemeManager.IsDark ? "#0a0d12" : "#f4f6fa")) !important;
        color-scheme: @(ThemeManager.IsDark ? "dark" : "light") !important;
    }

    :root {
        --z-chrome-edge: @(ThemeManager.IsDark ? "1px solid var(--mud-palette-divider)" : "none");
    }
</style>

@code {
    protected override void OnInitialized()
        => ThemeManager.OnChange += OnThemeChanged;

    // The main layout subscribes for its own reasons; the minimal and empty layouts do not, and a
    // theme switched from another tab has to reach them too.
    private void OnThemeChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
        => ThemeManager.OnChange -= OnThemeChanged;
}
